  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 207 PM CST Mon Nov 16 2026\n\nPatchy fog could try and develop early tomorrow morning just east of \nthe low cloud deck tonight over the Flint Hills. Meanwhile models in \ngood agreement with upper level ridge gradually shifting east for \nTuesday into Wednesday. Pronounced southwest flow aloft will then \noverspread the central plains this period, as main upper PV wave \nejects from the Rockies northeast into the northern plains. This \nwill cause a response in surface pressure gradient tightening up \nover Kansas thus boosting south wind speeds for Tuesday through \nWednesday. South winds could possibly reach advisory levels over a \nportion of central Kansas Tuesday afternoon even despite mixing \ndepth being reduced by low clouds. Temperatures gradually warm this \nweek with above normal readings and no precipitation expected as low \nlevel moisture remains scarce. South winds will ramp up again on \nFriday over Kansas. \n\nLong range models show a fleet footed upper PV wave racing east \nacross the region on Saturday. Once again precipitation chances look \nto be more limited this period with better chances for locations \neast of Kansas. This is due to later arrival time of deeper moisture \nfinally being encountered by the upper PV wave.",
